\begin_layout Title
Macroeconomic Theory II: Homework 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem

\emph on
No capital model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
No capital version of the RBC model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t},N_{t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\log C_{t}+\theta\log(1-N_{t})\right)\right]}
\]

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}=A_{t}N_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a stationary random variable, representing a technology shock.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive the first-order conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
How does 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 respond to a change in 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain your answer in (b).
 (HINT: Notice that the utility function is a King-Plosser-Rebelo type.)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We set up the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\log C_{t}+\theta\log(1-N_{t})+\lambda_{t}(A_{t}N_{t}-C_{t})\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

to derive the first-order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial C_{t}}:\ \frac{1}{C_{t}}-\lambda_{t}=0\iff\frac{1}{C_{t}}=\lambda_{t}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial N_{t}}:\ -\frac{\theta}{1-N_{t}}+\lambda_{t}A_{t}=0\iff\frac{\theta}{1-N_{t}}=\lambda_{t}A_{t}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the first-order conditions and the constraint, we can obtain an expression
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{\theta}{1-N_{t}}=\frac{A_{t}}{C_{t}}\\
\theta=\frac{1}{N_{t}}-1\\
N_{t}=\frac{1}{1+\theta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Therefore, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}=\frac{A_{t}}{1+\theta}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 does not appear in the equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 is independent of changes to 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall the general form of the King-Plosser-Rebelo utility function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(C,L)=\frac{1}{1-\sigma}\left\{ \left[C\nu(L)\right]^{1-\sigma}-1\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 denotes the intertemporal elasticity of substitution.
 So, the utility function in the problem description is obtain by taking
 the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\rightarrow1$
\end_inset

.
 This means that intertemporal consumption is unit elastic.
 Also, the KPR utility function implies exactly offsetting income and substituti
on effects of wage changes on labor.
 The marginal rate of substitution of leisure for consumption
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{u_{L}(t)}{u_{C}(t)}
\]

\end_inset

must grow at a constant rate and at the same rate as labor productivity
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial C_{t}}{\partial N_{t}}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\theta}{1-N_{t}}=A_{t}
\]

\end_inset

Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a random variable and represents techonology shock, so it does not grow.
 As a result, marginal product of labor does not experience constant grow,
 so neither should the MRS of leisure for consumption.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 should not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

, which coincides with our findings in part (b).
\end_layout

